    {
      "id": "navigation-about",
      "type": "page-settings",
      "label": "About this scheme",
      "position": {
        "x": 380,
        "y": -1000
      },
      "width": 520,
      "height": 320,
      "data": {
        "presentation": {
          "glyph": {
            "kind": "icon",
            "name": "Settings"
          },
          "baseLevel": "detail"
        },
        "title": "About this scheme",
        "rows": [
          {
            "label": "Status",
            "value": "Agreed and built. Seen running on 12 October 2026."
          },
          {
            "label": "Agreed",
            "value": "12 October 2026"
          },
          {
            "label": "Built so far",
            "value": "All seven steps. The node ladder, when browsing: a click comes to a node, then gives it focus at reading size, then enters it; a double-click enters at once. The tile ladder: a click frames a graph that is far, then enters it; a double-click enters at once; the title bar selects without entering. Escape and a click on empty space step out one level, from a node to its graph and from the graph to the workspace. The camera glides at each step. One history, on every account: it remembers the graph, the node and the distance, the address bar’s Back and Forward travel it, and a graph that is no longer open is fetched again. The trail in the workspace toolbar shows where you are and steps out to any level. Going through: a Portal already in focus is gone through on the next click, a double-click goes through from any distance, and Ctrl-click on a link in a page’s text opens the graph beside this one without travelling. Travel along edges: an edge inside a graph is framed, then selected, then travelled to the end you are not at; a port follows its edge; a trunk between two graphs is framed, then selected, then travelled; a handle travels to the graph it faces, and a port on a border to the node that owns it. Walking: with a node in focus, → walks along the edge leaving it and ← back again; where there is more than one way on, the edges are offered by name and ↑ and ↓ pick among them. Step 7: the drawn way-back Portals are gone from the demos and from the canon indexes. Since then: an edge’s name leads to the edge’s own graph, between graphs and inside one; and a link on a page you are reading answers a click."
          },
          {
            "label": "Open questions",
            "value": "Five, at the end"
          }
        ],
        "identity": {
          "graphId": "navigation-scheme"
        },
