    {
      "id": "navigation-rules",
      "type": "page-section",
      "label": "Five rules",
      "position": {
        "x": 380,
        "y": -640
      },
      "width": 520,
      "height": 320,
      "data": {
        "presentation": {
          "glyph": {
            "kind": "icon",
            "name": "Article"
          },
          "baseLevel": "detail"
        },
        "title": "Five rules",
        "body": "## 1. A click means closer\n\nIf the thing is far or small, the camera comes to it. If it is in view, it takes focus. If it already has focus, you go into it. The same three steps for a graph, a node and a Portal. A double-click goes straight to the last of them.\n\n## 2. Stepping out means further\n\nEscape, the Step out button, or a click on empty space takes you back one step: out of a node to its graph, out of a graph to the workspace. It is always the reverse of the click that got you there.\n\n## 3. Going through never closes anything\n\nFollowing a Portal or a link opens the other graph beside the one you are in, placed by its relation, and the camera travels to it. The graph you left stays open. Graphs are closed on purpose, never as a side effect. Without the workspace there is no “beside”: see “Without the workspace”.\n\n## 4. The app keeps the way back\n\nEvery move is remembered. Back returns you to exactly where you were: the same graph, the same node, the same distance. No graph has to draw a way back for you.\n\n## 5. A drag moves, a click travels\n\nDragging never navigates. It moves the thing, or pans the view if the thing is pinned or is empty space. Clicking never moves anything.",
        "identity": {
          "graphId": "navigation-scheme"
        },
        "classRef": "tlz://twilite.zone/library/templates/page-template/classes/page-section.node-class.node"
      },
